  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What to check first</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Customer details</strong>Names, contact details and account information need personal-data review.</li>
        <li><strong>Support tool terms</strong>Some help-desk platforms restrict reuse of ticket content. Check this first.</li>
        <li><strong>Completeness</strong>Tickets with a clear resolution are easier to use than open or half-finished ones.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What a resolved ticket teaches a model</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A closed ticket is a worked example: the problem as the customer described it, the checks that ruled the obvious causes out, and the fix that held. The gap between the reported symptom and the real cause is the part worth licensing.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Symptom against cause</strong>What the customer said was wrong, next to what was actually wrong.</li>
        <li><strong>The checks that ruled things out</strong>Diagnostic steps that produced a negative result, which are rarely written down anywhere else.</li>
        <li><strong>What the fix depended on</strong>Whether the answer was a setting, a refund, a code change or an escalation, and who could approve it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What keeps tickets out of scope</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Support archives carry the most personal data of any category here, so they are cut back more often than they are refused outright.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Card numbers pasted into a ticket</strong>Payment-card data is excluded under the buyer&rsquo;s published terms, and pasted details are removed before review.</li>
        <li><strong>Health details in a support thread</strong>Medical information is excluded unless there is a documented consent path.</li>
        <li><strong>Tickets with no outcome</strong>Open, abandoned or merged tickets show the problem but not the answer, so they add little.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

